          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A primigravida at 36 weeks is found to have a frank breech presentation on ultra</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A primigravida at 36 weeks is found to have a frank breech presentation on ultrasound with no contraindications to version. Which is the most appropriate initial management to offer?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Immediate emergency caesarean section</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Emergency caesarean is not indicated when there is no labour or fetal distress. ECV should be attempted first if there are no contraindications.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>External cephalic version (ECV) at 36–37 weeks</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. ECV is the recommended first-line intervention for uncomplicated breech, offering a window to convert to vertex before labour and avoid operative delivery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Planned vaginal breech delivery at term</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While vaginal breech delivery is possible in selected cases, the structured approach recommends attempting ECV first to convert to vertex, avoiding breech delivery risks altogether.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Induction of labour with oxytocin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Inducing labour with a persistent breech without first attempting version subjects mother and fetus to the risks of vaginal breech delivery unnecessarily.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is ECV. Antenatal management of breech aims to convert to vertex before labour, and ECV at 36–37 weeks is the recommended first step when no contraindications exist."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "ECV is the cornerstone of antenatal breech management — it offers a safe opportunity to convert to vertex before labour begins, and should be offered whenever no contraindications exist."
                }
              ],
