          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In confirmed obstructed labour, resuscitation and preparation for operative deli</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In confirmed obstructed labour, resuscitation and preparation for operative delivery should proceed:</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Sequentially — stabilise the patient fully before arranging surgery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Waiting to complete resuscitation before arranging surgery wastes critical time. The two must proceed in parallel because every minute of delay directly worsens outcomes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>In parallel — simultaneously, as every minute of delay worsens outcomes</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In obstructed labour, resuscitation (IV access, fluids, catheterisation) and operative preparation must occur simultaneously, because delay at any point worsens maternal and fetal outcomes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Only after a trial of oxytocin augmentation to confirm true obstruction</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Augmenting contractions with oxytocin in the presence of mechanical obstruction is dangerous — it increases the force against an impassable barrier and can precipitate uterine rupture.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Only after confirming fetal viability on cardiotocography</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While fetal monitoring is important, delaying operative intervention to complete a CTG trace when obstruction is confirmed would cost precious time. Management of obstruction is operative delivery, not further observation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct approach is parallel management. Resuscitation and preparation for operative delivery must proceed simultaneously; augmenting contractions in the presence of obstruction is dangerous and can precipitate rupture."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Simultaneous resuscitation and operative preparation is the fundamental principle of managing obstructed labour — time lost is outcome lost."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
