          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 28-year-old primipara undergoes her first caesarean section and wishes to atte</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 28-year-old primipara undergoes her first caesarean section and wishes to attempt vaginal birth in her next pregnancy. Which aspect of the LSCS technique is most critical to enabling this plan?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Choosing a Pfannenstiel over a Joel-Cohen skin incision</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The choice of skin incision affects cosmesis and possibly recovery speed, but it does not influence the strength of the uterine scar — which is what determines safety in future labour.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Achieving a strong uterine closure that produces a durable lower-segment scar</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. A well-closed lower-segment incision heals with a scar strong enough to withstand the forces of future labour, enabling safe trial of labour after caesarean (TOLAC).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Delivering the placenta by controlled cord traction rather than manual removal</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The method of placental delivery is a technical consideration, but it does not directly determine the strength of the uterine scar or the safety of future vaginal birth.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Closing the rectus sheath with interrupted rather than continuous sutures</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Abdominal wall closure technique affects wound strength but not the uterine scar. It is the uterine closure that determines the risk of rupture in subsequent labour.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is achieving a strong uterine closure. The narration emphasises that the goal of LSCS technique includes producing a scar capable of withstanding future labour — this is the enabling factor for vaginal birth after caesarean."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The uterine scar is the critical factor — the goal of LSCS technique is not just safe delivery now but a uterine closure capable of withstanding labour in future pregnancies, enabling VBAC."
                }
              ],
